# ---- the gh_rest ETag cache (optional — on by default) ---------------------
# bin/lib/gh-rest.sh (BH-1295) caches the ETag GitHub sends on a GET and sends
# it back as If-None-Match on the next identical read. An unchanged resource
# answers 304, which does not count against the App installation's rate limit
# — the merge conductor, reconcile-prs, merge-sweep and review-prs's approved-
# head retry all re-read the same PR tick after tick, and most of those reads
# come back unchanged. This is what stopped installation 161623167 (6,600
# req/h, shared by every hub lane and every pool machine) from exhausting
# itself twice in one day on 2026-09-21.
# GH_ETAG=0 is the kill switch: no If-None-Match header goes out, no cache
# file is read or written, and gh_rest behaves byte for byte as it did before
# this cache existed. Set it if a box's cache directory needs to be ruled out
# during an incident, or if a repo's ETags are misbehaving.
#GH_ETAG=1
# The cache lives at $HARNESS/state/gh-etag/, one file per (method, path,
# args) key. GH_ETAG_TTL_H drops an entry once it has gone this many hours
# without a fresh read; GH_ETAG_MAX caps the directory at this many entries,
# newest kept, oldest dropped, once the cap is hit. Both are read by the sweep
# that gh_rest_etag_report runs at most once an hour, so raising either is
# cheap and safe — the bound only exists to keep a box's disk from filling on
# an install nobody prunes.
#GH_ETAG_TTL_H=48
#GH_ETAG_MAX=20000
# GH_ETAG_MAX is an entry COUNT, not a byte bound — a diff/patch media-type
# read can store a whole PR's diff, so GH_ETAG_MAX_BYTES caps any single
# entry's body; a fresh read over the cap is never stored (and drops a
# smaller stale entry for the same key rather than leaving it to replay).
# Default 1 MiB, generous for a PR-sized diff.
#GH_ETAG_MAX_BYTES=1048576
